**Psoriasis** is a long-term skin condition where skin cells are made faster than usual, causing **patches of flaky skin**. It affects around **1 in 50 people** in the UK. It is linked to the immune system and often runs in families. It comes and goes, with flare-ups.

## Symptoms

- **patches (plaques)** of skin covered in **silvery scales**. They look red or pink on white skin, and purple or dark brown on black and brown skin
- often on the **elbows, knees, scalp and lower back**
- itching or soreness
- pitted or thickened **nails**

## Types of psoriasis

- **plaque psoriasis**, the most common type
- **scalp psoriasis**
- **guttate psoriasis**, small drop-shaped spots, often after a sore throat
- **inverse psoriasis**, in skin folds
- **pustular** and **erythrodermic** psoriasis, which are rare and can be serious

## Common triggers

- stress
- skin injury, such as cuts or scratches
- infections, such as throat infections
- smoking and alcohol
- some medicines

## Linked conditions

Up to **1 in 3** people with psoriasis get **psoriatic arthritis**. Psoriasis is also linked to **heart disease**, diabetes and depression.

{% callout type="danger" title="Get urgent help" %}
Call **NHS 111** or go to **A&E** if your skin suddenly becomes red or dark and inflamed **all over your body**, or you have widespread pus-filled spots and feel unwell.
{% /callout %}

Read about [psoriasis treatment](/conditions/skin/psoriasis/treatment/).
